diff --git a/packages/fcl-tls/README.md b/packages/fcl-tls/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..f5823f6312
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/fcl-tls/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,198 @@
+# Client TLS selection and native Windows TLS
+
+This increment adds a Windows Schannel client and an opt-in `smartsslsockets`
+unit. It targets this FPC 3.3.1 source branch. A pure Pascal macOS
+Network.framework client prototype and HTTP transport seam are implemented,
+with Darwin build/link evidence on both architectures; strict native TCP FIN
+truncation detection remains unresolved. It requires the explicit
+FPC_NETWORK_FRAMEWORK_NATIVE build define until macOS acceptance is complete;
+see [docs/macos-transport.md](docs/macos-transport.md).
+
+```pascal
+uses fphttpclient, smartsslsockets;
+var Client: TFPHTTPClient;
+begin
+  Client:=TFPHTTPClient.Create(nil);
+  try
+    Client.VerifySSLCertificate:=True;
+    Writeln(Client.Get('https://example.com/'));
+  finally Client.Free end;
+end.
+```
+
+On Windows, with no supported TLS DLL alongside the executable, this uses
+Schannel and, with verification enabled, Windows certificate trust without a third party TLS DLL.
+`schannelsslsockets` can also be used directly in place of `smartsslsockets`.
+
+## Selection contract
+
+Selection happens on the first `TSSLSocketHandler.GetDefaultHandlerClass` or
+`GetDefaultHandler` call. The selected class and any failure are cached under a
+critical section; later calls do not retry loading or change providers.
+
+1. A supported application-directory OpenSSL pair takes priority.
+2. If no pair member exists, application-directory GnuTLS is considered.
+3. If neither exists, Windows selects Schannel. With the explicit prototype
+   build define on supported 64-bit Darwin, available Network.framework is used.
+   Other targets or unavailable system APIs report no native adapter.
+
+Detection uses the executable's absolute directory, independently of the working
+directory and PATH. OpenSSL names follow `openssl.SSL_DLL_Names` and their matched
+crypto names on Windows; GnuTLS names are `libgnutls-30.dll`, then `libgnutls.dll`.
+Unix candidates are OpenSSL 3, 1.1, then unversioned library pairs, and GnuTLS
+30, then unversioned. Architecture and dependencies are checked by the platform
+loader; Windows also checks the PE machine before loading. Required client and
+verification exports must exist. An incomplete, corrupt, wrong-architecture or
+incompatible library is an error, not permission to move to a lower-priority
+backend. On Windows, safe DLL dependency-search flags must be supported by the OS.
+
+Configure overrides before the first factory call, including failed calls:
+
+```pascal
+SetSmartTLSBackend(tbSchannel); // bypass bundled libraries explicitly
+// Or:
+SetSmartTLSBackend(tbOpenSSL);
+SetSmartTLSLibraryPaths('C:\MyApp\libssl-3-x64.dll',
+  'C:\MyApp\libcrypto-3-x64.dll', '');
+```
+
+`GetSmartTLSBackend`, `TLSBackendName` and `GetSmartTLSLibraryPath` expose the
+selection without triggering it. Until successful selection, the backend is
+`tbAuto`. Configuration becomes immutable when selection starts. Explicit paths
+must be absolute and the OpenSSL pair must be supplied together. Missing
+explicit paths are errors. No environment variable implicitly changes policy.
+
+The existing explicit `opensslsockets` / `gnutlssockets` registration and usage
+remain available. `SetDefaultHandlerClass` clears the optional new lazy factory.
+The smart unit depends on its backends, so its initialization runs after theirs;
+its priority does not depend on the application's `uses` order.
+
+Smart selection owns TLS library initialization. Previously initialized bindings
+for a selected library are rejected. The new binding-level acquire APIs compare
+the pinned module handles under the same lock as legacy initialization. A
+competing initializer cannot silently substitute another module. Successful
+selection claims the binding until unit finalization: `DestroySSLInterface`
+returns false while claimed; `FreeGnuTLS` and loading a different GnuTLS path
+raise an exception. Existing explicit usage without a claim retains its behavior.
+The application must not release another owner's claim or directly overwrite
+global binding pointers.
+
+## Validation and client scope
+
+`VerifyPeerCert` and `fphttpclient.VerifySSLCertificate` keep the target
+branch's existing default, False. Set True explicitly for chain and hostname
+validation. False disables both checks for that connection; it does not change
+TLS protocol selection, encryption, backend selection or system security settings.
+`AfterSocketHandlerCreate` can override the handler option before connection.
+A custom `OnGetSocketHandler` result keeps the existing caller-owned setup rules.
+
+With True, Schannel uses automatic Windows trust/hostname validation. Smart
+OpenSSL installs its configured default CA paths, uses DNS or IP SAN matching as
+appropriate, and can also load the client's CA file/directory. Applications may
+need to supply OpenSSL CA material on Windows. GnuTLS uses its configured/system
+trust and enables hostname-aware handshake verification only with True. The
+smart GnuTLS verification-status check is likewise conditional. No provider
+fallback or implicit verification downgrade happens after TLS begins.
+
+The application callback retains its existing post-handshake semantics: it can
+reject an otherwise successful connection, but cannot rescue a handshake that
+already failed certificate verification. OpenSSL and Schannel invoke it after a
+successful handshake for either switch value; GnuTLS preserves its existing
+True-only callback behavior. This increment does not redefine it as a callback
+for individual validation errors.
+Client-only `Accept` fails explicitly. No application cryptography is implemented.
+
+Schannel supports the default OS protocol policy (`stAny`) and explicit TLS 1.2.
+It probes modern `SCH_CREDENTIALS` before using legacy credentials on systems
+that report those credentials unsupported, before any TLS handshake. This does
+not change FPC's OS compatibility baseline. Supported TLS versions depend on the
+OS policy; current tests cover Windows 10 x64 and TLS 1.2, not older Windows,
+Windows 11 TLS 1.3, 32-bit or ARM64.
+
+The first native client increment explicitly rejects client certificates,
+application CA files/directories, custom ciphers, ALPN and disabling SNI. These
+capabilities are not silently ignored. `CertificateData.HostName` remains the
+certificate-generation field; the optional Schannel `ServerName` property is
+the authenticated peer name when TCP connects to an IP or a tunnel. Otherwise
+the `TInetSocket` peer hostname is used. Callers should pass ASCII/IDNA hostnames.
+
+Schannel preserves incomplete TLS records, unconsumed encrypted bytes and
+decrypted leftovers. It handles `SEC_I_RENEGOTIATE` by returning to ISC with the
+same trust policy and preserves plaintext returned with renegotiation or
+`close_notify`. A TCP EOF without `close_notify` is a truncation error. Handshake
+uses `ConnectTimeout`, falling back to `IOTimeout`; send/receive operations use
+`IOTimeout`. The handler owns a nonblocking TCP transport and waits with the
+same socket deadline on partial sends/receives, including backpressure. Windows
+SSPI credential and trust calls are synchronous; socket deadlines do not cancel
+work inside those OS APIs. Closing has a bounded wait even if no I/O timeout was set. A handler
+has one owner: simultaneous operations on the same handler are not supported.
+
+## Build and reproduce
+
+Use the existing bootstrap compiler and GNU make from the FPC bin directory.
+FPC 3.2.2 cannot directly build the current `TNullPtr`-using library sources;
+build this branch's compiler and RTL first. Example from the source root:
+
+```powershell
+$env:PATH = 'C:\lazarus\fpc\3.2.2\bin\x86_64-win64;' + $env:PATH
+make -C compiler cycle FPC=C:/lazarus/fpc/3.2.2/bin/x86_64-win64/ppcx64.exe -j4
+make -C packages all FPC=D:/Projects/fpc/source/compiler/ppcx64.exe 'FPMAKEOPT=-T 4 -sap' -j4
+```
+
+The package build compiles unit targets for the current Windows target and skips
+example programs. It does not install anything. The test runner builds into the
+chosen output directory and preserves `results.log`:
+
+```powershell
+& .\packages\fcl-tls\tests\run.ps1 `
+  -OutputDirectory "$env:TEMP\fpc-native-tls-tests" `
+  -OpenSSLLibrary 'C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\libssl-3-x64.dll' `
+  -CryptoLibrary 'C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\libcrypto-3-x64.dll' `
+  -OpenSSLExecutable 'C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\openssl.exe' `
+  -OpenSSLCAFile 'C:\Program Files\Git\usr\ssl\certs\ca-bundle.crt' -Online
+```
+
+Omit `-Online` for tests without public-network access. Supply only already
+installed library fixtures; nothing is downloaded. The loader matrix creates
+isolated application directories. Supplying `-OpenSSLExecutable` adds 87 real
+local TLS tests even without `-Online`: temporary CA/leaf certificates, TLS
+1.2/1.3, DNS/IPv4/IPv6 identities, rejected chains and an intentionally truncated
+handshake. A Python SSL server binds loopback; its CA is supplied only in each
+child client's environment, not installed into any trust store. Test keys are
+disposable fixtures, not application credentials. The optional public runner
+temporarily sets the current PowerShell process's CA environment and restores it.
+
+`teststates.py` uses a test-only SSPI boundary
+provider, separately compiled, to test rare state transitions and send/close backpressure; it does not prove
+cryptography or real OS trust behavior. Public tests use real Schannel and retain
+system validation. The relay forwards ciphertext and splits TCP fragments; it
+does not decrypt traffic or change trust. A certificate test requires the expected
+Schannel validation code; connection errors/timeouts fail the test.
+
+Public EOF diagnosis and its bounded reproduction command are in
+[docs/openssl-eof.md](docs/openssl-eof.md). A diagnostic `probe` process may exit
+zero after collecting a failed handshake; its output never counts as a passing
+certificate test. No diagnostic configuration changes production selection or
+adds a retry/downgrade.
+
+See [docs/validation.md](docs/validation.md) for the observed results and gaps.
+
+## API references
+
+- [Microsoft Schannel credential policy](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/schannel/ns-schannel-schannel_cred)
+- [Microsoft Schannel decryption and renegotiation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthn/decryptmessage--schannel)
+- [Microsoft Schannel shutdown](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthn/shutting-down-an-schannel-connection)
+- [GnuTLS session verification](https://gnutls.org/manual/html_node/Core-TLS-API.html#gnutls_005fsession_005fset_005fverify_005fcert)
+
+### macOS connection layer status
+
+The pure Pascal provider, TLS stream/handler and fphttpclient pre-TCP factory
+are implemented without a C object dependency. Proxy/HTTPS Unix sockets/native
+SSE extraction and unsupported native TLS options fail explicitly. Portable
+models pass on Windows (HTTP 40, handler 22, stream 20); the shared callback
+model reports zero leaks. These are not macOS compile or TLS evidence.
+The Darwin prototype remains behind FPC_NETWORK_FRAMEWORK_NATIVE and mandatory
+system-symbol detection. Required Apple ABI/link/lifetime/trust tests and the
+official bootstrap proposal are documented in the
