+# Pure Pascal Network.framework client prototype
+
+The connection layer, SSL handler and fphttpclient connection factory are now
+implemented in Pascal. No C object or third party TLS library is a runtime
+dependency of the native backend. Both Darwin architectures have compiled,
+linked and passed construction on macOS 15.7.9. Real TLS acceptance is under
+review, including a TCP FIN truncation limitation. It remains opt-in with
+FPC_NETWORK_FRAMEWORK_NATIVE;
+without that define smart Darwin selection reports native unavailable.
+
+## Connection and ownership
+
+Network.framework owns TCP and TLS. fcl-net's TClientConnection is a TStream
+with Connect, Close, CanRead, BytesAvailable, timeout and connection-state APIs.
+TSSLSocketHandler.CreateClientConnection is called before any TInetSocket is
+constructed. Its default nil result preserves the existing socket path. A
+nonnil result takes ownership of its handler; an exception leaves ownership
+with the caller. The native wrapper frees the stream and handler once.
+
+fphttpclient reads/writes through the selected transport and preserves handler
+setup/callback precedence, the False verification default and keep-alive
+endpoint/protocol checks. Closed transports are still freed by the owner.
+ExtractSocket rejects a native transport. Protected ExtractClientConnection
+transfers its ownership and removes callbacks into the former HTTP client.
+Proxy connections, HTTPS Unix sockets and native SSE socket extraction fail
+explicitly before requests are sent. The legacy plaintext Unix socket path is
+retained. Native custom credentials/trust files, ALPN, ciphers and protocol
+selection are unsupported and fail before creating a connection. The peer is
+the actual logical connection host; CertificateData.HostName remains the
+certificate-generation field, whose legacy default is localhost.
+
+Applications serialize Connect and ordinary IO. Close may interrupt one active
+call. Before Free they must coordinate other callers so no new/queued call can
+enter. Stream and wrapper destruction cancel and drain the full active operation
+before freeing its handler/locks. This is not unrestricted concurrent use.
+Unix programs use cthreads first (or an equivalent thread manager).
+
+## ABI and native lifetimes
+
+networkframeworkcommon defines the nine-function provider contract.
+networkframeworkapi declares Network/Security/dispatch functions and FPC cblocks.
+Apple C bool uses a one-byte Boolean, not this branch's four-byte ctypes.cbool.
+networkframeworknative supplies the provider and bounded synchronous adapter;
+networkframeworktls supplies the stream; networkframeworksslsockets owns the
+stream and handler. No handwritten cryptography is present.
+
+The loader resolves mandatory APIs from fixed system framework/libSystem paths
+under a one-time lock. Missing frameworks/symbols make native unavailable.
+Handles remain for the process lifetime because callbacks can finish late.
+The native harness Mach-O audit found no strong Network/Security or third-party
+TLS imports; FPC Blocks ISA symbols resolve through libSystem. The branch's default deployment targets
+(x86_64 10.8, arm64 11.0) include Blocks, but this work does not establish every
+custom older deployment setting. Network.framework is checked at runtime;
+the branch baseline is not raised. iOS/iphonesim are excluded. Secure Transport
+for older macOS remains a separate decision.
+
+A dedicated serial dispatch queue runs asynchronous state/send/receive blocks.
+FPC method blocks capture a TMethod; copying a block does not retain its Self.
+Separate state/send/receive context references keep Self alive. Callbacks write
+only internal 16 KiB buffers, never caller buffers or a Pascal stream owner.
+Send data is copied by dispatch_data_create with its default nil destructor.
+Close publishes cancellation, wakes waiters and cancels the connection; pending
+callbacks retain their contexts until completion. Close is cancellation, not a
+claim of graceful TLS close_notify. A receive error takes priority over EOF,
+and already-delivered plaintext precedes a deferred error. On macOS 15.7.9,
+Network.framework reports a bare TCP FIN without TLS close_notify as error-free
+EOF, just like clean TLS closure. The strict truncation test remains failed;
+the native backend does not currently distinguish these closures. CanRead timeout is benign; connect/read/write
+deadlines are terminal. Mach monotonic time drives native deadlines.
+
+VerifyPeerCert=True retains system trust and logical hostname validation.
+False installs a per-connection accept-peer verification block. Neither changes
+TLS protocol policy, system trust/settings or the chosen backend. The existing
+application callback remains a post-handshake veto and cannot rescue failed
+platform verification. There is no backend retry after connection begins.
+
+## Reproducible validation
+
+Windows run.ps1 compiles model tests into separate output directories. The
+shared native-state model uses FPC_NETWORKFRAMEWORK_MODEL; this define compiles
+shared production state helpers without compiling Darwin cblocks. The 20 stream,
+40 HTTP and 22 handler checks and the shared callback model pass. The latter
+records 146 allocations/frees and zero unfreed blocks. Windows package rebuild
+and the complete local 87-case TLS regression pass. These prove neither Apple
+SDK declarations nor Darwin code generation, trust or runtime behavior.
+
+The native harness tests/macos/testnetworknative.pp supports construction
+without TCP, trusted ready, exact-domain/code rejection, 1 MiB fragmented echo,
+idle deadlines, concurrent Close/CanRead cancellation, clean EOF and TCP abort.
+Only an explicit TLS certificate-domain/code may satisfy a certificate case;
+an arbitrary network error must fail. FPC_NETWORKFRAMEWORK_DIAGNOSTICS adds
+atomic live/destroyed context counters, and each native test waits up to five
+seconds for all pending contexts to drain. These counts observe context release,
+not every system allocation or sanitizer result.
+
+The sibling testnetworkhttp.pp and testnetworktls.py generate disposable private
+server certificates with an existing OpenSSL CLI, use existing Python ssl only
+as the TLS server and exercise Pascal clients with no third-party TLS binding
+loaded. The matrix records TLS1.2/1.3, False/default/after-create, True rejection,
+application vetoes, HTTP connection counts, partial 1 MiB echo, terminal deadlines,
+concurrent cancellation, clean EOF, RST and bare FIN separately. Private roots
+remain untrusted, so their wrong-name/expired leaves do not isolate those failures.
+Public example.com/badssl controls use unchanged system trust. Every attempt and
+error remains in results.json; there are no retries that turn failures into passes.
+
+With a branch-built Darwin compiler/RTL, typical flags are:
+
+```sh
+ppcx64 -n -dFPC_NETWORK_FRAMEWORK_NATIVE -Fu<source>/rtl/units/x86_64-darwin \
+  -Fu<source>/packages/fcl-tls/src -FU<output> -FE<output> \
+  -XR<existing-Apple-SDK> <source>/packages/fcl-tls/tests/macos/testnetworknative.pp
+```
+
+Use ppca64 and aarch64-darwin for arm64. Keep the architecture-specific package
+paths and package/native compile defines consistent. Do not link the earlier
+C bridge or set FPC_NETWORK_FRAMEWORK_BRIDGE.
+
+The standalone tests/macos/run.sh accepts an existing branch-built compiler
+and a fresh absolute output directory. It uses the existing Apple SDK and does
+not provision software or certificates in system trust:
+
+```sh
+bash packages/fcl-tls/tests/macos/run.sh /absolute/path/to/compiler/ppca64 /tmp/fpc-native-tls-fresh
+```
+
+CI orchestration belongs solely to
+[ACTom/fpc-macos-ci](https://github.com/ACTom/fpc-macos-ci), outside this source
+patch. Manual runs use the approved official 3.2.2 DMG only as a temporary
+bootstrap: read-only mount, pkgutil extraction, no installer scripts, then build
+the pinned branch compiler and RTL. The owner approved at most 20 cumulative
+workflow executions, including prior attempts; the two-architecture matrix counts
+once. Each repeat requires diagnosis and a reviewed exact carrier SHA.
+
+## Historical first preflight
+
+[Run 37319310587](https://github.com/ACTom/fpc-macos-ci/actions/runs/37319310587)
+tested the earlier C exploration on x86_64/arm64 macOS 15.7.9, SDK 15.5,
+clang 17. Fixed-patch application, Apple API/Blocks construction and production
+C object compilation passed. Overall it failed at a C harness warning under
+-Werror. Neither runner had FPC. That evidence does not apply to the new Pascal
+Darwin branch. The C exploration was archived locally for comparison and is
+excluded from the final FPC patch; no corrected C runtime acceptance is claimed.
+
+## References
+
+- [Apple Network.framework](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/network)
+- [Apple Security protocol headers](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/Security/blob/main/protocol/SecProtocolOptions.h)
+- [Apple libdispatch data contract](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/libdispatch/blob/main/dispatch/data.h)
+- [Clang Blocks ABI](https://clang.llvm.org/docs/Block-ABI-Apple.html)
+
+## Official bootstrap preflight outcome
+
+[Run 37332944563](https://github.com/ACTom/fpc-macos-ci/actions/runs/37332944563)
+tested carrier a870a1e2793b886660557e9572489478ef6b6ec5, patch digest
+fbb3f5b5cac2fe3ced1938474a2d7a759ef610d4d4478031a7e8171a6b62e17a.
+Both standard runners finished with failure on macOS15.7.9 / Xcode16.4.
+Fixed-base patch application, official DMG download, hdiutil image verification
+and pkgutil extraction passed. Both observed the same DMG SHA256:
+05d4510c8c887e3c68de20272abf62171aa5b2ef1eba6bce25e4c0bc41ba8b7d.
+This is the observed digest, not independent publisher-signature evidence.
+The image contains an mpkg wrapper with one nested flat pkg; that payload was
+extracted without installer scripts. Native compiler and matching RTL paths
+were found on both architectures. No compiler was installed globally.
+
+The bootstrap script incorrectly passed `lipo -verify_arch <arch> <file>`;
+lipo treated the trailing filename as another architecture and exited 1.
+The correction is `lipo <file> -verify_arch <arch>`, following the actual tool's
+usage. Subsequent runs validated the corrected bootstrap hello on both architectures.
+Version/target queries, bootstrap hello, compiler cycle/RTL, branch Blocks,
+native compile/link/construction/imports and package/models were NOT RUN.
+No native TLS connection occurred in this historical run. Subsequent diagnosed
+runs under the owner's expanded authorization are recorded separately. Text evidence
+was retained for each architecture. Existing Windows evidence remains unchanged.
+
+## Darwin build and initial TLS evidence
+
+Run 37336482398 passed the official bootstrap hello, branch compiler cycle/RTL
+and three branch Blocks ABI checks on both architectures. Native compilation
+failed at Assigned(cblock); the required completion is now called directly
+inside the foreign exception boundary.
+
+Run 37338838447 (carrier 2770bcdabbf8f3b449261f9303ac1d90d0a70479) passed native
+compile/link, construction/context drain, Mach-O imports and complete packages
+on both architectures. The load-command minimums remain 10.8 (x86_64) and 11.0
+(arm64); this is a linker audit, not old-system runtime evidence. A standalone
+model compile then failed due to missing rtl-extra BSD include paths in the CI
+command; package compilation itself passed.
+
+Run 37340068774 adds those declared include paths and native TLS fixtures.
+Its first ARM results establish actual Pascal HTTPS, False/True behavior and
+pending-context release. Strict TCP FIN rejection fails because the native
+framework returns clean EOF. Final per-architecture acceptance results must be
+read independently; Windows tests supply no Apple runtime evidence.
