+# OpenSSL public EOF investigation
+
+This follow-up distinguishes a certificate rejection from a connection that ends
+before certificate processing. The initial public online run remains failed;
+EOF is never considered a successful negative certificate test.
+
+## Bounded experiment
+
+On 2026-10-05, `untrusted-root.badssl.com` resolved here to `104.154.89.105`.
+All configurations used the same installed Git OpenSSL 3.5.4 DLLs and CA bundle,
+explicit hostname verification and certificate verification. Every probe was a
+new process; no backend retried or changed protocol after failure.
+
+| Configuration | Trial 1 | Trial 2 |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Independent openssl s_client, default | EOF, handshake read 0 bytes | EOF, handshake read 0 bytes |
+| FPC smart, default protocol | X.509 19, certificate verify failed | EOF, X.509 default 0, inactive TLS |
+| Independent s_client, X25519 group | EOF, read 0 bytes | X.509 19, read 2927 bytes |
+| Independent s_client, explicit TLS 1.2 | X.509 19, read 2935 bytes | X.509 19, read 2935 bytes |
+| FPC smart, explicit TLS 1.2 | X.509 19, inactive TLS | X.509 19, inactive TLS |
+
+The default CLI ClientHello was about 1.5 KiB; the X25519 CLI hello was about
+330 bytes. The shorter hello still failed once, so these observations do not
+establish a ClientHello-size cause. The successful certificate-processing paths
+correctly rejected a self-signed certificate in the supplied chain (19).
+
+The independent CLI reproduces the failure without any FPC code, and in the
+zero-read failures there is no peer certificate to authenticate. A displayed
+`Verify return code: 0` in an incomplete handshake is not authenticated success.
+This excludes the FPC-specific handler path as a necessary cause of the symptom.
+It does not identify whether the remote endpoint, an intermediate network
+component or an OpenSSL interoperability interaction causes the termination.
+There is no packet capture or server-side trace proving the exact external cause.
+
+The original diagnostic logs are in the task's
+`tls-build/public-eof-probe/results.json` and `variants.json`. They retain complete
+CLI handshake state/message output and FPC verification/active-state output.
+Public diagnostics are collected separately from the strict regression runner.
+
+The checked-in `testpublicprobe.py` was subsequently run once to verify its
+reproduction path. The independent CLI default, X25519 and explicit TLS 1.2
+probes all reached certificate processing and rejected the chain (19); the FPC
+default probe also rejected it (19), while the FPC explicit TLS 1.2 probe ended
+with EOF, default verification result 0 and inactive TLS. Its complete record is
+`tls-build/public-probe-repro/results.json`. Thus explicit TLS 1.2 is not a
+reliable workaround either. The collection command exited 0, which confirms
+diagnostic collection only, not a passing certificate-test run.
+
+## Reproduce without changing trust settings
+
+First build with `tests/run.ps1`, supplying the existing OpenSSL pair and
+`-OpenSSLExecutable`. Then collect up to two rounds of public diagnostics:
+
+```powershell
+python .\packages\fcl-tls\tests\testpublicprobe.py `
+  "$env:TEMP\fpc-native-tls-tests\testopensslpeer.exe" `
+  'C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\openssl.exe' `
+  'C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\libssl-3-x64.dll' `
+  'C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\libcrypto-3-x64.dll' `
+  'C:\Program Files\Git\usr\ssl\certs\ca-bundle.crt' `
+  "$env:TEMP\fpc-public-eof-probe" --trials 2
+```
+
+Each subprocess has its own CA environment. This command collects diagnostics;
+an exit zero means collection completed, not that a failed TLS handshake passed
+validation. Public conditions can differ on another run.
+
+## Independent local evidence
+
+`testlocaltls.py` creates fresh disposable CA/leaf certificates and runs a Python
+SSL server exclusively on loopback. It covers fixed TLS 1.2/1.3, automatic
+protocol negotiation with a TLS1.2-only server, DNS/IPv4/IPv6 SANs, expired,
+self-signed and unknown-root chains, complete untrusted chains, fphttpclient,
+and deliberate TCP FIN before any certificate. All 28 cases passed.
+
+Certificate cases require exact X.509 errors plus the validation-failure text.
+The deliberate EOF has X.509 default 0, a failed handshake and inactive TLS.
+No failure is rescued by a callback, backend fallback or validation downgrade.
+The full regression runner with local fixtures exited 0; the prior public failure
+remains recorded in [validation.md](validation.md).
+
+These experiments demonstrate that the current client validates the controlled
+chains and distinguishes truncation correctly. No TLS runtime change is justified
+by the available evidence. This follow-up changes only tests and documentation.
+
+## References
+
+- [OpenSSL CA validity controls](https://docs.openssl.org/3.5/man1/openssl-ca/)
+- [OpenSSL s_client diagnostics](https://docs.openssl.org/3.5/man1/openssl-s_client/)
